# [K65] REGRESSION — ON A VM ROUTE WITH NO DFA SCAN IN FRONT, THE
# NECESSARY-BYTE PRE-CHECK TESTS EVERY MEMBER OF THE NECESSARY SET, SO WHETHER
# A HOSTILE SUBJECT ANSWERS NOMATCH OR GIVES UP NO LONGER DEPENDS ON WHICH
# MEMBER THE PICK CHOSE (docs/dev/known_issues.md K65; docs/spec/tuning.md
# §2.29; fix shape (a), D123 addendum 8 item 1).
#
# The pattern's backreference declines the hybrid, so the artifact is a
# FRAMED VM program (`RX_VM_PREFILTER "none"`) and the pre-check is the only
# linear no-match proof it has. Its necessary set is {Z, @}; the pick is `Z`
# under `encoding byte` (the frequency prior) and `@` under `encoding utf8`
# (the rightmost fallback). On a leading class run of length L, `([a-z]+)+`
# backtracks exponentially before the absent byte fails the attempt, so with
# ONE member tested each block GAVE UP (`steps`, driver exit 3) on the
# subjects lacking the OTHER member: the `...Zb` cells under byte (Z present,
# @ absent) and the `...@b` cells under utf8. Every `n` cell below is NOMATCH
# in PCRE2 and must be NOMATCH here under either encoding.
#
# Block 3 is the RUN form of the same defect: `@#` is a necessary run, the run
# check tests it and nothing else, so a subject holding the run and lacking
# `Z` gave up under both encodings.
#
# The `gu` cells are the CONTROL that the budget really reaches the VM: with
# every necessary byte present no pre-check can help, and the same run still
# exhausts the block's budget, as PCRE2 does past its own match limit. They
# prove the `n` cells pass because of the pre-check, not because 10000 steps
# happened to be enough. `budget steps=10000` and L = 16..18 keep the failing
# direction to milliseconds and let python `re` (which backtracks the same
# way) verify every cell. (tests/codegen/run_prechecks.sh §5.8 asserts the
# emitted all-members text; this file asserts the answers.)

pattern (x?)([a-z]+)+Z.@\1
features backrefs
encoding byte
budget steps=10000
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@b"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@b"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@b"
m "abcZx@" 0 6
gu steps "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb!@"

pattern (x?)([a-z]+)+Z.@\1
features backrefs
encoding utf8
budget steps=10000
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@b"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@b"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@b"
m "abcZx@" 0 6
gu steps "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb!@"

pattern (x?)([a-z]+)+Z.@#\1
features backrefs
encoding byte
budget steps=10000
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@#"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@#"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa@#"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZ#"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZ#"
n "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZ#"
m "abcZx@#" 0 7
gu steps "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaZb!@#"
